course: dictionary
lesson: 1140
title: Bribe: buying a decision
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bribe is money or a gift given to someone to make them do something dishonest or unfair, such as break a rule. The words around it give you clues: «Giving gifts for votes is a bribe» tells you that the gifts are wrong because they are used to win votes unfairly.
explain: «Fair voting needs a secret ballot, slips of paper in a box, so nobody votes just to please a best friend, and no lollies for votes, because that is a bribe.» The lollies are the clue: a sweet offered to change how someone votes is a bribe. Check by swapping in your meaning: "a gift to make someone vote unfairly" still makes sense.
explain: Bribe can also be a verb. To bribe someone means to offer them money or a gift to make them do what you want, often wrongly. You will meet the phrases take a bribe, offer a bribe and bribe someone with. In family life, a treat can be joked about as a bribe, but that is informal.
fact: A bribe is money or a gift given to make someone do something dishonest or unfair.
fact: As a verb, to bribe someone means to offer them money or a gift to make them do what you want, often wrongly.
fact: Bribing a judge or a police officer is a crime.
fact: «In 1856 the Australian colonies of Victoria and South Australia began using secret printed ballots, so voters could not be bullied or bribed.»
fact: Informally, a treat given to get a child to do something can be called a bribe, as a joke: «a sweet as a bribe».
exercise: «Giving gifts for votes is a bribe, and in real elections paying or gifting people for votes is against the law in most countries.» What does bribe mean here? | A prize given to the best voter | A secret printed ballot | *Money or gifts given to win votes unfairly | A law that all countries have agreed on
exercise: «In 1856 the Australian colonies of Victoria and South Australia began using secret printed ballots, so voters could not be bullied or bribed.» Why did they use secret ballots? | To give children a vote | To count the votes faster on a computer | *So voters could not be bullied or bribed | To let everyone see who people voted for
exercise: Mum says, «It's a sweet as a bribe to get you to tidy your room.» What is she doing? | Buying a new cupboard | Asking you to share your sweets | Giving you a sweet for tidying well | *Offering a treat to get you to do something
test: «Fair voting needs a secret ballot, slips of paper in a box.» What is a secret ballot? | A sweet given to a friend | A law about bribes | A loud shout in the school hall | *Slips of paper in a box, so nobody can see how you vote
test: What does «to bribe someone» mean? | *To offer money or a gift to make them do what you want, often wrongly | To give a prize to the winner of a race | To count the votes after an election | To ask a question politely
exam: «Nobody votes just to please a best friend, and no lollies for votes, because that is a bribe.» Why is a lolly for a vote called a bribe? | It is the name of a sweet shop | It is a kind of ballot paper | It tastes nice, so it is a treat | *It is a gift that tries to buy a vote unfairly
